{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01UdqqQaMunW9q4uPGQxn7xv","type":"tool_result","content":"28\tuse std::io::{self, Read, Write};\n29\t\n30\t/// Minimal emulation of C's `scanf(\"%d\")` conversion reading from stdin.\n31\t///\n32\t/// `scanf` treats the input as a single stream and freely reads across newline\n33\t/// boundaries, but it only ever pulls in as much as the conversion needs (one\n34\t/// stdio buffer at a time) and stops the moment it sees a character that cannot\n35\t/// extend the conversion, pushing that character back.  The scanner below\n36\t/// mirrors that: it reads on demand into a fixed buffer and inspects the next\n37\t/// byte with `peek` (never consuming it unless it is part of the conversion), so\n38\t/// an unbounded stdin such as `/dev/zero` terminates immediately just as the C\n39\t/// program does instead of being slurped into memory.\n40\t///\n41\t/// Leading whitespace is skipped, an optional sign is accepted, and then a run\n42\t/// of decimal digits is consumed.  A conversion that finds no digits is a\n43\t/// matching failure and leaves the destination variable untouched (which is\n44\t/// exactly what the C program relies on for its `int x = 0, y = 0;` defaults).\n45\tstruct Scanner<R: Read> {\n46\t    inner: R,\n47\t    buf: [u8; 4096],\n48\t    /// Number of valid bytes in `buf`.\n49\t    len: usize,\n50\t    /// Read cursor into `buf`.\n51\t    pos: usize,\n52\t    /// Sticky: once the stream reports end-of-file (or an error, which behaves\n53\t    /// like end-of-file here) no further reads are attempted.\n54\t    eof: bool,\n55\t}\n56\t\n57\timpl<R: Read> Scanner<R> {\n58\t    fn new(inner: R) -> Scanner<R> {\n59\t        Scanner {\n60\t            inner,\n61\t            buf: [0u8; 4096],\n62\t            len: 0,\n63\t            pos: 0,\n64\t            eof: false,\n65\t        }\n66\t    }\n67\t\n68\t    /// Look at the next byte of the stream without consuming it (`ungetc`-like)